# Flash messages

A flash message is a short note that survives one redirect: "Settings
saved" after a form post, or "That link has expired" after sending someone
back to the start. The handler doing the work puts the message on its
redirect, and the page the browser lands on shows it once.

`howdy/flash` keeps the messages in a signed cookie, so they need no
storage on the server and work without JavaScript.

## Put a message on a redirect

Make one `Flash` when the app starts, from a secret of at least 32 bytes
that stays the same across restarts and on every server:

```gleam
import howdy/env
import howdy/flash

let assert Ok(secret) = env.get("SECRET_KEY_BASE")
let flash = flash.new(secret:)
```

After a form post, answer `303` so that refreshing the next page does not
post the form again, and put the message on that response:

```gleam
fn save_settings(ctx: Context, flash: flash.Flash) {
  use fields <- form.read(ctx)
  // ... save them ...
  controller.status(ctx, 303)
  |> response.set_header("location", "/settings")
  |> flash.put(flash, flash.Success, "Settings saved")
}
```

There are three levels, `Info`, `Success` and `Danger`. Put more than one
message and they are shown in the order they were put.

## Show it once

The page reads the messages with `flash.take`, which clears them on its
response, so a refresh does not show them again:

```gleam
fn settings_page(ctx: Context, flash: flash.Flash) {
  use messages <- flash.take(ctx, flash)
  let w = words.english()
  page.new("Settings")
  |> page.body([
    settings_form(),
    toast.region(w, [], toast.flashed(w, messages)),
  ])
  |> page.respond(ctx)
}
```

`toast.flashed` from `howdy_ui` turns each message into a toast that fades
on its own; the text is always shown as text, never as HTML. The region
and the close buttons are labelled with the [words](/docs/i18n/overview#Howdy-UI)
you give them, here English; the messages are yours, so write them in the
visitor's language. A page that redirects again without showing anything
should not take the messages: they stay in the cookie for the page after.

A live view can show them too: read them in the handler that serves the
page, pass them to the view, and add them to its toast queue with
`toast.push_flashed`.

## Why signed

The messages are signed with a key derived from the secret, so a page only
ever shows messages your app wrote. A cookie someone else planted, such as
from a sibling subdomain, or one edited by hand, is ignored and cleared.
They are not encrypted: never put anything secret in a flash message.

The cookie is called `howdy_flash`, lasts ten minutes, and uses
`cookie.defaults()`, so it is `Secure`, `HttpOnly` and `SameSite=Lax`.
Browsers accept `Secure` cookies from `http://localhost`; for plain HTTP on
another host, use `flash.cookie_options` with `cookie.secure(False)`.

## Flash or notification?

A flash message is feedback on something the user just did, shown on the
very next page. To tell someone about something that happened elsewhere,
such as another user's comment, use [notifications](/docs/notifications/overview),
which are kept until read and reach pages that are already open.

## Testing

`testing.cookies` gives the cookie a response set, and `testing.cookie`
sends it with the next request, as a browser would:

```gleam
pub fn saving_says_so_test() {
  let saved = testing.post_form("/settings", []) |> testing.send(app())
  let assert Ok(value) = testing.cookies(saved) |> list.key_find("howdy_flash")
  let page =
    testing.get("/settings")
    |> testing.cookie("howdy_flash", value)
    |> testing.send(app())
  assert string.contains(testing.text(page), "Settings saved")
}
```
